{#- "Agent benchmark" tab: one agent vs several, how it was tested and how the pipeline was improved.
    Every number comes from ab = agents_benchmark.build() (the benchmark result files). -#}
{% set A = ab.by %}
{% set m4 = A.runs %}{% set f4 = A.runs_first_attempt %}{% set m12 = A.runs_load12 %}
{% set s4 = A.runs_structured %}{% set s12 = A.runs_structured_load12 %}
{% set P = {} %}{% for x in ab.pipeline %}{% set _ = P.update({x.arm: x}) %}{% endfor %}
<style>
.ab-k{display:inline-block;width:11px;height:11px;border-radius:50%;margin-right:6px;vertical-align:-1px}
.ab-B{background:var(--s1)}.ab-O{background:var(--s4)}.ab-C{background:var(--s3)}.ab-D{background:var(--s2)}.ab-S{background:var(--s7)}
.ab-bars{display:grid;gap:5px;margin:8px 0}
.ab-row{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(120px,210px) 1fr 74px;gap:10px;align-items:center;font-size:.82rem}
.ab-row .lab{color:var(--ink2)}.ab-row .val{text-align:right;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;color:var(--ink)}
.ab-row .track{background:var(--chip);border-radius:4px;height:14px;overflow:hidden}
.ab-row .fill{height:100%;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0}
@media (max-width:640px){.ab-row{grid-template-columns:1fr 64px;row-gap:2px}.ab-row .lab{grid-column:1/-1}}
.ab-grp{font-size:.78rem;font-weight:600;color:var(--muted);margin:12px 0 2px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.04em}
.ab-steps{counter-reset:s;list-style:none;padding:0;margin:0;display:grid;gap:12px}
.ab-steps>li{counter-increment:s;position:relative;padding-left:44px;max-width:100ch}
.ab-steps>li::before{content:counter(s);position:absolute;left:0;top:0;width:30px;height:30px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--s1);color:#fff;font-weight:700;display:grid;place-items:center;font-size:.9rem}
.ab-steps h3{margin:.15em 0 .3em}
.ab-opts{grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,250px),1fr))}
.ab-opts .card h3{margin:.1em 0 .4em;font-size:1rem}.ab-opts .card p{margin:.35em 0;font-size:.87rem;color:var(--ink2)}
.ab-res{font-weight:600;color:var(--ink)!important}
pre.ab-code{background:var(--chip);border-radius:8px;padding:10px 12px;font-size:.76rem;overflow-x:auto;white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;margin:.5em 0}
.ab-yes{color:var(--good);font-weight:700}.ab-no{color:var(--bad);font-weight:700}
</style>
{% macro bars(rows, max, fmt) -%}
<div class="ab-bars">{% for r in rows %}
<div class="ab-row"><span class="lab"><span class="ab-k ab-{{ r[0] }}"></span>{{ r[1] }}</span>
<span class="track"><span class="fill ab-{{ r[0] }}" style="display:block;width:{{ [100 * r[2] / max, 0.6] | max }}%"></span></span>
<span class="val">{{ fmt.format(r[2]) }}</span></div>{% endfor %}</div>
{%- endmacro %}

<section class="panel" id="tab-agents" data-name="agents">
{{ plain('agents') }}
<h2>One agent or a team of agents?</h2>
<p class="lede">This atlas is written by a team of AI agents: five specialists gather evidence with their own tools and an
orchestrator writes the report. A team costs more calls than one agent, so two experiments tested whether it is worth
it, and a third round fixed what the experiments showed was wrong. This tab explains every step in plain words.</p>

<div class="kpis">
  <div class="kpi"><b>{{ s4.arms.D.accuracy }} %</b><span>team answers correct after the fix (was {{ m4.arms.D.accuracy }} %)</span></div>
  <div class="kpi"><b>{{ s4.arms.D.failed + s12.arms.D.failed }}</b><span>team calls with a broken answer after the fix, of {{ s4.arms.D.calls + s12.arms.D.calls }} (before: {{ f4.arms.D.failed }} of {{ f4.arms.D.calls }} on the first try)</span></div>
  <div class="kpi"><b>{{ P.multi_improved.runs | map(attribute='checklist_score') | join(" and ") }}</b><span>facts of {{ ab.checklist_max }} in the improved team's reports (one agent: {{ P.single.runs | map(attribute='checklist_score') | join(" and ") }})</span></div>
  <div class="kpi"><b>${{ "%.2f" | format(P.multi_improved.cost) }}</b><span>per full analysis with the improved team (one agent: ${{ "%.2f" | format(P.single.cost) }})</span></div>
  <div class="kpi"><b>{{ "%.0f" | format(P.multi_improved.minutes) }} min</b><span>per full analysis with the improved team (one agent: {{ "%.0f" | format(P.single.minutes) }} min)</span></div>
</div>

<div class="howbox"><h4>The short answer</h4><ul>
<li><strong>When the evidence is already collected</strong> and only needs reading, one agent is enough. A team only
matched it after its answers were forced into a checked format, and it still costs two to two and a half times as much.</li>
<li><strong>When the evidence has to be found</strong> with dozens of database queries, as in this atlas, the team wins:
after the improvements it writes reports as complete as one agent's, for about {{ "%.0f" | format(100 * (1 - P.multi_improved.cost / P.single.cost)) }} % less money and about
{{ "%.0f" | format(P.single.minutes / P.multi_improved.minutes) }} times faster, because the specialists search in parallel on a cheaper model.</li>
</ul></div>

<h2>Experiment 1: reading evidence that is already collected</h2>
<p class="lede">Here every option received the same evidence and had to draw the same conclusions from it. This isolates
the reasoning: no searching, no tools.</p>
<ol class="ab-steps">
<li><h3>Build the test cases</h3>
<p>Twelve genes from the atlas (CA9, CD70, NNMT, SLC16A3, ENPP3, HAVCR1, COL23A1, RAB42, PSAT1, PCK1, CD74, HK2) were
turned into 24 evidence files ("dossiers"). Each holds 13 to 18 real records about one gene from 11 kinds of data:
tumour vs normal RNA and protein, a second patient cohort, single-cell data, tumour purity, cell-surface location,
staining in normal organs, the normal-tissue proteome, cancer cell-line dependency, survival and two drug trials.</p>
<p>Three things make the cases hard on purpose:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Names are hidden.</strong> Each gene becomes a code such as <code>ENTITY_96</code>, so the model cannot answer from
memory and has to read the records.</li>
<li><strong>Look-alike records.</strong> Each dossier also holds 12 records about two other hidden genes, which must be
ignored.</li>
<li><strong>Altered copies.</strong> Twelve of the 24 dossiers are copies with one decisive record changed (for example a
protein that no longer goes up, or a safety concern added), so the right answer changes. These copies are synthetic
tests, not biology.</li>
</ul>
<p>One record looks like this:</p>
<pre class="ab-code">{"type": "treatment_within_arm", "study": "CheckMate025", "arm": "nivolumab_cm025", "log_hr_per_sd": 0.057, "padj": 0.922, "entity": "ENTITY_96"}</pre></li>

<li><h3>Ask the same seven questions, with written rules</h3>
<p>For each dossier the options answer six yes / no / not-enough-data questions and give a verdict:</p>
<div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Rule in short</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Is the protein up in tumours?</td><td>Yes if the tumour-vs-normal protein change is significant and positive.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Does it replicate?</td><td>Yes if a second cohort (TCGA or the confirmatory protein cohort) shows the same direction.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Is it made by tumour cells?</td><td>Yes if tumour cells hold the largest share of its transcripts and the change survives in a pure tumour.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Is it on the cell surface?</td><td>Yes if the surface annotation says surface.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Is there a safety concern in vital organs?</td><td>Yes if staining in a vital organ is at least moderate or the normal proteome marks it as enriched there.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Do cancer cells depend on it selectively?</td><td>Yes if kidney-cancer cell lines need it clearly more than other cancer lines.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Verdict</strong></td><td>Surface target candidate, loss biomarker, infiltration signal or needs more data, from the six answers.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Missing data always means "not enough data", never "no". The rules are given to every option, so the test measures
whether they apply them correctly, not whether they agree with our taste.</p></li>

<li><h3>Compare four options</h3>
<div class="grid ab-opts">
<div class="card"><h3>A. Rules, no AI</h3><p>A short program applies the written rules exactly.</p>
<p>It cannot be wrong by construction, so it is not a competitor: <strong>its answers are the answer key</strong> the
others are marked against.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3><span class="ab-k ab-B"></span>B. One agent</h3><p>One AI reads the whole dossier and answers all
seven questions.</p><p class="ab-res">{{ m4.arms.B.accuracy }} % correct, ${{ "%.3f" | format(m4.arms.B.cost_case) }} per dossier.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3><span class="ab-k ab-C"></span>C. Specialists only</h3><p>Four AIs split the data (expression;
cells; safety; dependency and clinic). Each sees only its own records and answers only its own questions. A fixed rule
combines their answers into the verdict; nobody looks at the whole.</p>
<p class="ab-res">{{ m4.arms.C.accuracy }} % correct, ${{ "%.3f" | format(m4.arms.C.cost_case) }} per dossier.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3><span class="ab-k ab-D"></span>D. Specialists + orchestrator</h3><p>The same four specialists, then
an orchestrator reads the whole dossier and their answers, checks them and gives the final answers. This is the atlas's
own design.</p><p class="ab-res">{{ m4.arms.D.accuracy }} % correct, ${{ "%.3f" | format(m4.arms.D.cost_case) }} per dossier.</p></div>
</div>
<p class="small">All options used the same model (Sonnet 5.5, medium effort) with no tools, web access or memory.
Option D's cost includes the specialists it relies on.</p></li>

<li><h3>Run it several times, in two sizes</h3>
<p>Each option answered every dossier three times (three replicates) with 4 dossiers per call, and again with 12
dossiers per call (about 360 records at once) to see whether a long context hurts. Two altered versions of the same
gene were never in the same call. A call whose answer could not be read was retried once; results are shown both
before and after that retry.</p></li>

<li><h3>Mark the answers</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Verdict accuracy:</strong> share of dossiers with the correct verdict. A lost answer counts as wrong.</li>
<li><strong>Confident errors:</strong> a firm yes or no where the key says otherwise. These are worse than saying "not
enough data".</li>
<li><strong>Cost and time:</strong> the API price and the clock time reported by Claude Code for every call.</li>
<li><strong>Uncertainty:</strong> differences between options come with a 95 % interval, resampling by gene so that two
versions of one gene are not counted as independent.</li>
</ul></li>
</ol>

<h3>Results</h3>
<div class="grid two">
<div class="card"><h4 style="margin:.1em 0">Verdicts correct</h4>
<div class="ab-grp">4 dossiers per call, first attempt</div>
{{ bars([("B", ab.arms.B, f4.arms.B.accuracy), ("C", ab.arms.C, f4.arms.C.accuracy), ("D", ab.arms.D, f4.arms.D.accuracy)], 100, "{} %") }}
<div class="ab-grp">4 dossiers per call, after one retry</div>
{{ bars([("B", ab.arms.B, m4.arms.B.accuracy), ("C", ab.arms.C, m4.arms.C.accuracy), ("D", ab.arms.D, m4.arms.D.accuracy)], 100, "{} %") }}
<div class="ab-grp">12 dossiers per call, after one retry</div>
{{ bars([("B", ab.arms.B, m12.arms.B.accuracy), ("C", ab.arms.C, m12.arms.C.accuracy), ("D", ab.arms.D, m12.arms.D.accuracy)], 100, "{} %") }}
<p class="small">Always guessing the most common verdict would score {{ m4.majority }} %.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h4 style="margin:.1em 0">Price per dossier</h4>
<div class="ab-grp">4 dossiers per call</div>
{{ bars([("B", ab.arms.B, m4.arms.B.cost_case), ("C", ab.arms.C, m4.arms.C.cost_case), ("D", ab.arms.D, m4.arms.D.cost_case)], 0.06, "${:.3f}") }}
<div class="ab-grp">12 dossiers per call</div>
{{ bars([("B", ab.arms.B, m12.arms.B.cost_case), ("C", ab.arms.C, m12.arms.C.cost_case), ("D", ab.arms.D, m12.arms.D.cost_case)], 0.06, "${:.3f}") }}
<p class="small">US dollars of API use. Time per dossier, 4 per call: B {{ "%.1f" | format(m4.arms.B.wall_case) }} s, C {{ "%.1f" | format(m4.arms.C.wall_case) }} s, D {{ "%.1f" | format(m4.arms.D.wall_case) }} s.</p></div>
</div>

<h3>What the errors were</h3>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h4 style="margin:.1em 0">1. Broken answers</h4>
<p class="small" style="color:var(--ink2)">Each option must reply in a fixed format (JSON) that a program reads. When the reply
breaks the format the program cannot read it, so every case in it is lost, even if the reasoning was right. Real
examples: a stray <code>&lt;/br&gt;</code> or an extra <code>}</code> inside the reply, two replies in one, or cases
left out. First attempts that broke: B {{ f4.arms.B.failed }} of {{ f4.arms.B.calls }} calls, C {{ f4.arms.C.failed }} of {{ f4.arms.C.calls }}, D {{ f4.arms.D.failed }} of {{ f4.arms.D.calls }} (4 per call).
More calls means more chances to break: a team makes four or five calls where one agent makes one.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h4 style="margin:.1em 0">2. Reasoning with a narrow view</h4>
<p class="small" style="color:var(--ink2)">Specialists that see only their slice made real mistakes, some of them confident
(a wrong firm yes or no): {{ "%.2f" | format(m4.arms.C.confident_errors) }} per dossier with 4 per call and {{ "%.2f" | format(m12.arms.C.confident_errors) }} with 12. In option C a fixed rule
passes such a mistake straight into the verdict. Option D's orchestrator, which sees everything, corrected every one it
saw: it never made a reasoning error.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h4 style="margin:.1em 0">3. One weak link</h4>
<p class="small" style="color:var(--ink2)">In option D everything ends in one orchestrator call. When that call broke, all
dossiers in it were lost at once. That is why D, which reasoned perfectly, still scored {{ m4.arms.D.accuracy }} % instead of 100 %.</p></div>
</div>
<p><strong>Conclusion of experiment 1:</strong> one agent was right every time and was the cheapest. The team's losses came
mostly from broken answers, and partly from specialists reasoning without the full picture.</p>

<h2>Experiment 2: the real pipeline, where the evidence has to be found</h2>
<p class="lede">In real use nobody hands the evidence over. The agents query the atlas's databases (gene changes,
single-cell data, trials, drug screens, literature and more: about 50 tools) and write the full report.</p>
<ol class="ab-steps">
<li><h3>Two set-ups, same everything else</h3>
<p><span class="ab-k ab-D"></span><strong>Team</strong> (the published <code>/informe</code>): an orchestrator (Opus 5.5) launches five
specialists (Sonnet 5.5, a cheaper model) at the same time; each searches its own area and writes its findings; the
orchestrator checks them and writes the report.</p>
<p><span class="ab-k ab-S"></span><strong>One agent</strong> (<code>/informe_single</code>): one Opus 5.5 agent does all five specialists' work itself, one
query after another, with the same tools, then writes the report.</p>
<p>Same question, data, tools and rules; two runs each, alternated.</p></li>
<li><h3>Mark the reports</h3>
<p>An open report has no single right answer, so four measures were fixed before the runs:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Automatic verification.</strong> Every sentence must cite evidence records; a checker confirms each record
exists, belongs to that gene and matches every number quoted. A sentence that fails is "unsupported".</li>
<li><strong>Coverage.</strong> How many kinds of evidence and how many records the report cites.</li>
<li><strong>A checklist of {{ ab.checklist_max }} facts</strong> the data establish and a complete report should state (list below).</li>
<li><strong>Cost, time and number of steps</strong> from Claude Code's own records.</li>
</ul></li>
</ol>

<h3>Results</h3>
<div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>Set-up</th><th>Run</th><th class="num">Unsupported sentences</th><th class="num">Checklist (of {{ ab.checklist_max }})</th><th class="num">Cost</th><th class="num">Time</th><th class="num">Steps</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for arm in ["multi", "single"] %}{% for r in P[arm].runs %}
<tr><td><span class="ab-k ab-{{ 'D' if arm == 'multi' else 'S' }}"></span>{{ "Team" if arm == "multi" else "One agent" }}</td><td>{{ loop.index }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.unsupported }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.checklist_score }}</td><td class="num">${{ "%.2f" | format(r.cost_usd) }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.duration_min }} min</td><td class="num">{{ r.turns }}</td></tr>
{% endfor %}{% endfor %}
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Both set-ups passed verification with no unsupported sentence. One agent cost about
{{ "%.0f" | format(100 * (P.single.cost / P.multi.cost - 1)) }} % more and took {{ "%.0f" | format(P.single.minutes / P.multi.minutes) }} times as long, because it does some 250 steps one after another
where the specialists work in parallel. But one agent stated slightly more of the checklist ({{ P.single.runs | map(attribute='checklist_score') | join(" and ") }} against
{{ P.multi.runs | map(attribute='checklist_score') | join(" and ") }}).</p>

<h2>How the team was improved</h2>
<p class="lede">The goal: keep the team's speed and low cost, and make its answers as good as one agent's.</p>
<ol class="ab-steps">
<li><h3>Fix the broken answers: a checked format</h3>
<p>Each call now gives Claude Code a schema of the reply: which case ids must appear, exactly once each, and which
answers are allowed. The reply is checked before it comes back, so it cannot be malformed or leave a case out.
Experiment 1 was then run again with the same cases, model and batches.</p></li>
<li><h3>Find why the team's reports missed facts</h3>
<p>Reading the reports showed the specialists <em>had</em> found the missing facts (HK2 failing to replicate, the CheckMate 025
trial, the PRISM drug screen) but the orchestrator left them out when writing. The team's reports cited only
{% for r in P.multi.runs %}{{ (100 * r.finding_evidence_cited_share + 0.001) | round | int }} %{{ " and " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %} of the evidence its specialists had collected; one agent's cited
{% for r in P.single.runs %}{{ (100 * r.finding_evidence_cited_share + 0.001) | round | int }} %{{ " and " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}.</p></li>
<li><h3>Make sure key findings reach the report</h3>
<ul>
<li>Each specialist marks its three to five <strong>key findings</strong>, especially negative ones: a change that does not
replicate, no selective dependency, no difference between trial arms, a signal from immune cells rather than tumour
cells, a safety concern.</li>
<li>A new check (<code>scripts/coverage.py</code>) lists every key finding the report does not cite. The orchestrator must
add it or explain in the limitations why it left it out.</li>
<li>The writing instructions now name the newest data (CheckMate 025, the normal-tissue proteome, PRISM), the
replication check runs for every top-10 gene, and every candidate that is stable in the ranking must appear in the
target list or be explained.</li>
</ul>
<p>These are general rules about not losing findings, not answers to the checklist.</p></li>
<li><h3>Try a cheaper orchestrator</h3>
<p>The orchestrator (Opus) was about 60 % of the team's cost, so the improved team was also run with Sonnet as
orchestrator.</p></li>
</ol>

<h3>Results after the improvements</h3>
<div class="grid two">
<div class="card"><h4 style="margin:.1em 0">Experiment 1 again, with the checked format</h4>
<div class="ab-grp">4 dossiers per call</div>
{{ bars([("B", ab.arms.B, s4.arms.B.accuracy), ("C", ab.arms.C, s4.arms.C.accuracy), ("D", ab.arms.D, s4.arms.D.accuracy)], 100, "{} %") }}
<div class="ab-grp">12 dossiers per call</div>
{{ bars([("B", ab.arms.B, s12.arms.B.accuracy), ("C", ab.arms.C, s12.arms.C.accuracy), ("D", ab.arms.D, s12.arms.D.accuracy)], 100, "{} %") }}
<p class="small">Broken answers: {{ s4.arms.D.failed + s12.arms.D.failed }} of {{ s4.arms.D.calls + s12.arms.D.calls }} team calls and {{ s4.arms.B.failed + s12.arms.B.failed }} of {{ s4.arms.B.calls + s12.arms.B.calls }} single-agent calls; no retry was needed.
Price per dossier (4 per call): B ${{ "%.3f" | format(s4.arms.B.cost_case) }}, C ${{ "%.3f" | format(s4.arms.C.cost_case) }}, D ${{ "%.3f" | format(s4.arms.D.cost_case) }}.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h4 style="margin:.1em 0">The real pipeline, average of two runs</h4>
<div class="ab-grp">Checklist facts (of {{ ab.checklist_max }})</div>
{{ bars([("D", "Team, before", P.multi.checklist), ("D", "Team, improved", P.multi_improved.checklist), ("O", "Improved, Sonnet orchestrator", P.multi_improved_sonnet.checklist), ("S", "One agent", P.single.checklist)], ab.checklist_max, "{:.1f}") }}
<div class="ab-grp">Cost per analysis</div>
{{ bars([("D", "Team, before", P.multi.cost), ("D", "Team, improved", P.multi_improved.cost), ("O", "Improved, Sonnet orchestrator", P.multi_improved_sonnet.cost), ("S", "One agent", P.single.cost)], 7, "${:.2f}") }}
<div class="ab-grp">Minutes per analysis</div>
{{ bars([("D", "Team, before", P.multi.minutes), ("D", "Team, improved", P.multi_improved.minutes), ("O", "Improved, Sonnet orchestrator", P.multi_improved_sonnet.minutes), ("S", "One agent", P.single.minutes)], 50, "{:.1f}") }}
</div>
</div>

<div class="tw" style="margin-top:14px"><table><thead><tr><th>Set-up</th><th class="num">Unsupported sentences</th><th class="num">Checklist per run</th><th class="num">Evidence from specialists cited</th><th class="num">Cost per run</th><th class="num">Time per run</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for x in ab.pipeline %}
<tr><td>{{ x.label }}</td><td class="num">{{ x.runs | map(attribute='unsupported') | join(" · ") }}</td><td class="num">{{ x.runs | map(attribute='checklist_score') | join(" · ") }}</td>
<td class="num">{% for r in x.runs %}{{ (100 * r.finding_evidence_cited_share + 0.001) | round | int }} %{{ " · " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}</td>
<td class="num">{% for r in x.runs %}${{ "%.2f" | format(r.cost_usd) }}{{ " · " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}</td>
<td class="num">{% for r in x.runs %}{{ r.duration_min }} min{{ " · " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}</td></tr>
{% endfor %}</tbody></table></div>

<div class="howbox"><h4>What the improvements achieved</h4><ul>
<li><strong>No more broken answers.</strong> With the checked format the team (option D) answered every dossier correctly,
like one agent. The specialists-only option also improved, to {{ s4.arms.C.accuracy }} %.</li>
<li><strong>Reports as complete as one agent's.</strong> The improved team stated {{ P.multi_improved.runs | map(attribute='checklist_score') | join(" and ") }} of the {{ ab.checklist_max }} facts
(one agent: {{ P.single.runs | map(attribute='checklist_score') | join(" and ") }}), with no unsupported sentence. Read by hand: both reports say HK2 does not
replicate and use CheckMate 025 and PRISM; one of them misses that CD70 is partly made by T cells.</li>
<li><strong>Still cheaper and faster.</strong> ${{ "%.2f" | format(P.multi_improved.cost) }} and {{ "%.0f" | format(P.multi_improved.minutes) }} minutes per analysis, against ${{ "%.2f" | format(P.single.cost) }} and
{{ "%.0f" | format(P.single.minutes) }} minutes for one agent. The coverage check adds about {{ "%.0f" | format(100 * (P.multi_improved.cost / P.multi.cost - 1)) }} % to the team's earlier cost.</li>
<li><strong>The cheaper orchestrator did not pass.</strong> With Sonnet as orchestrator the analysis cost ${{ "%.2f" | format(P.multi_improved_sonnet.cost) }}, but its reports
ended with {{ P.multi_improved_sonnet.runs | map(attribute='unsupported') | join(" and ") }} unsupported sentences: it did not repair the specialist findings the checker
rejected, which Opus always did. The orchestrator therefore stays on Opus.</li>
</ul></div>

<details style="margin:14px 0"><summary><strong>The {{ ab.checklist_max }} checklist facts, run by run</strong></summary>
<div class="tw" style="margin-top:8px"><table><thead><tr><th>Fact</th>{% for r in ab.pipe_runs %}<th class="num">{{ r.run }}</th>{% endfor %}</tr></thead><tbody>
{% for k in ab.checks %}<tr><td>{{ ab.check_labels.get(k, k) }}</td>{% for r in ab.pipe_runs %}<td class="num">{% if r.checklist[k] %}<span class="ab-yes">✓</span>{% else %}<span class="ab-no">✗</span>{% endif %}</td>{% endfor %}</tr>
{% endfor %}</tbody></table></div>
<p class="small">multi = team before, opt = improved team, cheap = improved team with a Sonnet orchestrator, single = one agent.
The facts are found by text patterns (and, for PRISM, by the records cited), checked by reading the reports.</p></details>

<div class="note"><strong>Limits.</strong> Two runs per set-up in the real pipeline, so small differences are noise.
One agent was not re-run with the new writing instructions, so part of the gain may come from naming the newest data
rather than from the coverage check. Experiment 1 gives the rules explicitly and uses one model; it measures careful
reading, not open-ended scientific judgement, and its altered dossiers are synthetic. Model use for these tests cost
about ${{ "%.0f" | format(ab.spend_v2) }} (experiment 1, all conditions) and ${{ "%.0f" | format(ab.spend_pipe) }} (pipeline runs). Nothing here
validates any target.</div>
